  "history": "Few English words have changed as much as nice. It comes from Latin nescius, ‘not knowing’ (ne ‘not’ + scīre ‘to know’). In Old French, nice meant ‘silly, simple’, and when English borrowed it around 1300, it meant ‘foolish’ or ‘stupid’. Then the meaning moved, step by step: ‘shy’, ‘fussy’, ‘very exact’ (we still speak of a nice distinction, a very fine difference), ‘delicate’, and finally, in the 1700s, ‘pleasant’. By the 1800s a nice person was a kind person. Today nice is so common that teachers often ask students to try other words: lovely, kind, pleasant, delicious. Science comes from the same Latin verb scīre.",
  "historyEinfach": "Nice is a word that changed a lot. It comes from Latin. There it meant ‘not knowing’. 700 years ago, a nice person was a stupid person! Then the meaning changed again and again. About 300 years ago, nice started to mean ‘pleasant’. Today a nice person is a kind person.",
